  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1102 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA period of quiet weather expected across the Borderland for the\nnext week. Models in good agreement in transitioning from a\ncurrent northwest flow to a persistent westerly flow pattern. This\nwill keep moisture out of the area with just an occasional breezy\nday. For this evening, winds will gradually decrease as upper low\nmoves into the Central/Southern Plains and gradient relaxes. Much\nlower dew points will be moving in so mostly clear skies by mid\nevening. One small concern is potential for fog in the morning due\nto recent rains, but winds will not go completely calm and could\njust result in very patchy fog. Looks like a good hard freeze in\nthe Mimbres River Valley of the Gila region where lows in the mid\nto upper 20s look likely. \n\nFor Tue, a shortwave ridge moves into the west with lighter winds\nand mostly sunny skies. H85 temps warm to around 16-18C and with\nfull sun, should get good mixing. NBM temps looked about 3-5\ndegrees too cool for highs and MET/MAV guidance also agree with\nhighs being warmer. So did go ahead and raise highs closer to MOS\nguidance numbers. Same thing for Wed, which should have even\nbetter mixing with west winds increasing to 10-15mph. NBM temps\nseemed about 5 degrees too cool and again warmed up closer to MOS\nguidance.  \n\nThere will be a shortwave moving well to our north Wed night into\nThu which will cool temps down a few degrees. NBM temps looked\nmore reasonable here and didn't adjust much. Persistent weather\ninto the weekend as an upper low cuts off of Baja and low level\neasterly flow keeps temps near to slightly below normal.",
